WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.732
Facilities planning starts with what people need to do
in a space.

2
00:00:03.932 --> 00:00:13.779
Our schematic places a quiet-use area away from active
collaboration, keeps a clear route from the entrance,
and identifies flexible furniture and access to power.

3
00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:20.040
It illustrates relationships, not a construction-ready
design or a certified accessible layout.

4
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:27.823
An architectural program is a written description of
required spaces, uses, capacity, equipment, and
relationships.

5
00:00:28.023 --> 00:00:31.502
It gives design professionals a clear account of service
needs.

6
00:00:31.702 --> 00:00:34.664
It is not a computer program or a finished drawing.

7
00:00:34.864 --> 00:00:37.932
Library staff and users contribute experience.

8
00:00:38.132 --> 00:00:43.325
Facilities personnel, architects, and other specialists
contribute technical expertise.

9
00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:47.872
Managers connect that work to the service purpose and
available resources.

10
00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:56.654
A major project moves from needs and justification
through funding, design, implementation, and evaluation
after occupancy.

11
00:00:56.854 --> 00:01:04.731
Consider both initial project funding and continuing
operating costs, such as staffing, utilities, cleaning,
and replacement.

12
00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:09.748
A beautiful room that is difficult to maintain or use
has not fulfilled the plan.

13
00:01:09.948 --> 00:01:14.299
Flexible, durable choices can help the space adapt as
needs change.

